This guide uses a graduate thesis at Northeast Normal University to show how to typeset bilingual front matter, chapters and references with Typst. Starting from the minimal nenust example, authors select the degree type, enter thesis information and use labels to refer to sections, equations, figures and tables. The template generates the covers, contents and numbering, so adding a chapter does not require manually renumbering later references.

The bundled bibst module reads a single BibTeX database and supports both numeric and author-date citations under GB/T 7714—2025. Examples show how to group sources, cite specific pages, produce full references in footnotes and control the display of URLs, persistent identifiers and backlinks. When a different citation style is required, Typst can instead use its native processor to format the citations and bibliography according to an ordinary CSL style file.

The same tutorial source produces previews for different degrees, an anonymized double-sided version and both citation styles. Comparing these PDFs helps authors spot pagination and citation problems. Authors must still verify their sources, check for identifying information and review the submission copy against current university and departmental requirements.
